<Ui>
    <!--
        benilla's stable-master window (decision 1676) — the hunter's three-slot pet stable, dressed
        from the real 1.12 `PetStable.xml`/`PetStable.lua`. Every rule below is cited back to that
        extraction: `xml l.N` is `ref-PetStable.xml`, `lua l.N` is `ref-PetStable.lua`, and
        `GlobalStrings l.N` is the shipped `GlobalStrings.lua`.

        **The Lua lives here, inline.** The reference splits it (`<Script file="PetStable.lua"/>`,
        xml l.3); benilla ships one file per window, so the transcription sits in the CDATA block
        below in the reference's own order (OnLoad, OnEvent, Update, NoPetsAllowed).

        **An NPC session, reached through gossip**, and it behaves like every other one here
        (TrainerFrame.xml/MerchantFrame.xml's shape): a left-slot UIPanel (registered in
        UiPanels.xml, the reference's own `UIParent.lua:39` row), a "npc" portrait bake, and an
        `OnHide` that ends the session. The one thing that differs from its neighbours is that the
        close sends NO PACKET: `ClosePetStables()` is purely client-side (there is no close opcode
        in 5875 — `benilla-ui/src/script/stable.rs`'s own note), so the hide just clears the app's
        session.

        **Three slots, indices 0..2.** `NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS = 2` (lua l.1) is the count of *stable*
        slots a hunter can buy; slot 0 is the pet at their side, which is why every loop below runs
        `1..NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS` for the stabled pair and treats 0 separately.

        Named deferrals / deviations, each re-cited at its own site below:

          - **The model pane is the reference's own `<PlayerModel name="PetStableModel">`**
            (xml l.182-280), and so are the four scripts on it: `Model_OnLoad`, `Model_OnUpdate`
            and the two `Model_Rotate*` the buttons call (UIParent.xml). It was a plain `<Frame>`
            sampling a booth bake through `BenillaSetBoothTexture`, with the rotate quartet borrowed
            from our CharacterFrame.xml — both went when the character window migrated (decision
            1751): the widget draws its own booth now, and the yaw the app mirrors is the pane's
            own `SetRotation` state.
            One thing the reference's pane does that ours still does not: `DISPLAY_SIZE_CHANGED` →
            `this:RefreshUnit()` (xml l.196-200) is a no-op here, because the pane holds the unit
            TOKEN and the app re-resolves it every frame — there is no cached appearance to
            invalidate (`script/modelframe.rs`'s `RefreshUnit`).
          - The pane's booth is the `"stable"` slot in `crates/benilla-app/src/portrait`, joined to
            the pane by name in that module's `MODEL_PANE_BOOTHS`. The app resolves
            `GetSelectedStablePet()`'s slot into either the live pet's entity or — for a stabled or
            dismissed pet, which has no world object at all — the creature query's
            `CreatureDisplayInfo` id, and the booth mirrors whichever it got.
          - **`GetPetIcon()` does not exist in this client** (reference/1.12-globals.tsv has it as
            an engine global; benilla has never needed it). `PetStable_Update` calls it at lua l.51
            and l.161-162 and it is transcribed verbatim rather than guarded: the window's current
            pet slot cannot paint without it, and a guard here would hide a missing binding instead
            of reporting it.
          - `parent="UIParent"` (xml l.72) is ours too, and it attaches for real: the loader does
            read a cross-file `parent=`, resolving the name by global lookup at load (decision
            1734), and the frame below declares it. The frame's own `<Anchors>` are kept verbatim even
            though `ShowUIPanel` re-`SetPoint`s it into the left slot — they name the same
            TOPLEFT (0,-104) the slot manager writes, so the two agree (ItemTextFrame.xml keeps its
            reference anchor for the same reason).
          - `PetStableMoneyFrame`'s instance `<OnLoad>SmallMoneyFrame_OnLoad()</OnLoad>` (xml
            l.360-362) is dropped, and the cost frame's OnLoad keeps BOTH calls. benilla's
            `SmallMoneyFrameTemplate` already wires `SmallMoneyFrame_OnLoad` as its own OnLoad
            (MoneyFrame.xml) where the reference's template does not, and an instance `<Scripts>`
            block SetScripts over the inherited handler rather than adding to it — so the purse
            wants no OnLoad at all, and any instance that needs `MoneyFrame_SetType` must re-call
            the loader beside it. That is the idiom AuctionFrame.xml and MailFrame.xml already use.
          - Two housekeeping fixes inside `PetStable_Update`, both behaviour-identical: `background`
            is a LOCAL here (lua l.76 leaks it as a global), and the reference's `numSlots`/`numPets`
            locals (lua l.69-70) are dropped because nothing reads them — the loop asks
            `GetNumStableSlots()` directly, exactly as the reference's own l.79 does.
          - The GlobalStrings the window reads are declared below with the `X = X or "..."` idiom
            this codebase uses: the real localized file loads off the patch chain before our own
            files (`ui_script/lifecycle.rs`'s `load_global_strings`) and always wins; the fallbacks
            are what make the strings resolve in the test harnesses, which load no chain. They
            matter at LOAD, not just at runtime: a `text=` attribute is a global-string lookup
            performed while the frames below are being built.
    -->
    <Script><![CDATA[
        -- ── GlobalStrings this window uses (values + line cites from the shipped file) ───────────
        STABLES = STABLES or "Stables"                                    -- :4189
        CURRENT_PET = CURRENT_PET or "Current Pet:"                       -- :881
        STABLED_PETS = STABLED_PETS or "Stabled Pets:"                    -- :4188
        EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT = EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT or "Empty Stable Slot"      -- :1434
        STABLE_SLOT_TEXT = STABLE_SLOT_TEXT
            or "Do you wish to purchase another stable slot?"             -- :4190
        COSTS_LABEL = COSTS_LABEL or "Cost:"                              -- :870
        PURCHASE = PURCHASE or "Purchase"                                 -- :3128
        PET_DIET_TEMPLATE = PET_DIET_TEMPLATE or "Diet: %s"               -- :3037
        UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE = UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE or "Level %d"           -- :4436

        -- lua l.1. The count of STABLE slots (the two a hunter buys), not of window slots: the
        -- buttons run 0..2 with 0 being the pet at the player's side, which is why the loops below
        -- iterate 1..NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS and handle 0 on its own.
        NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS = 2

        -- ── The window ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        -- lua l.3-9, verbatim. UNIT_PET is here because a pet summoned or dismissed while the
        -- window is open changes slot 0 without the server sending a stable list.
        function PetStable_OnLoad()
            this:RegisterEvent("PET_STABLE_SHOW")
            this:RegisterEvent("PET_STABLE_UPDATE")
            this:RegisterEvent("PET_STABLE_UPDATE_PAPERDOLL")
            this:RegisterEvent("PET_STABLE_CLOSED")
            this:RegisterEvent("UNIT_PET")
        end

        -- lua l.11-32, all four arms. NOTE the SHOW arm's shape: `ShowUIPanel` can REFUSE (dead
        -- player, a native-center frame holding the screen — UiPanels.xml's `AdmitPanel`), and the
        -- reference reads that refusal back off `IsVisible()` and ends the session rather than
        -- leaving one open behind a window nobody can see. Ours can refuse for the same reasons and
        -- has the same session to end (`crate::ui_stable`'s), so the check is load-bearing here
        -- too, not defensive.
        function PetStable_OnEvent()
            if event == "PET_STABLE_SHOW" then
                ShowUIPanel(this)
                if not this:IsVisible() then
                    ClosePetStables()
                    return
                end
                PetStable_Update()
            elseif event == "PET_STABLE_UPDATE" or (event == "UNIT_PET" and arg1 == "player") then
                PetStable_Update()
            elseif event == "PET_STABLE_UPDATE_PAPERDOLL" then
                -- So warlock pets don't show (lua l.23-27, the reference's own comment). A warlock
                -- standing at a stable master has a pet but no pet UI, and the window must not
                -- offer to stable it.
                if UnitExists("pet") and not HasPetUI() then
                    PetStable_NoPetsAllowed()
                    return
                end
                SetPetStablePaperdoll(PetStableModel)
            elseif event == "PET_STABLE_CLOSED" then
                HideUIPanel(this)
            end
        end

        -- lua l.34-209 — the whole repaint, in the reference's own order and with its own
        -- structure. Read it as five passes: the portrait, the warlock gate, the auto-selection,
        -- the two stabled slots, the current-pet slot, and the purchase row.
        function PetStable_Update()
            -- Set stablemaster portrait (lua l.35-36): the "npc" unit token, the same live model
            -- bake every other NPC-session window binds.
            SetPortraitTexture(PetStableFramePortrait, "npc")

            -- So warlock pets don't show (lua l.38-46, the reference's own comment). NOTE this arm
            -- reads `isHunterPet`, the SECOND return, where the paperdoll arm above reads the
            -- first: a warlock's minion answers hasPetUI true and isHunterPet false, and it is the
            -- second that decides whether this window has anything to do.
            local hasPetUI, isHunterPet = HasPetUI()
            if UnitExists("pet") and not isHunterPet then
                PetStable_NoPetsAllowed()
                PetStableCurrentPet:Disable()
                return
            else
                PetStableCurrentPet:Enable()
            end

            -- If no selected pet try to set one (lua l.48-63). The -1 sentinel IS the API
            -- (`GetSelectedStablePet` returns it, and `stable.rs` defaults its selection to it for
            -- exactly this branch): a freshly opened window picks the current pet if there is one,
            -- otherwise the first occupied stable slot, so the pane is never blank when it has
            -- something to show.
            local selectedPet = GetSelectedStablePet()
            if selectedPet == -1 then
                if GetPetIcon() then
                    selectedPet = 0
                    ClickStablePet(0)
                else
                    for i = 0, NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS do
                        if GetStablePetInfo(i) then
                            selectedPet = i
                            ClickStablePet(i)
                            break
                        end
                    end
                end
            end

            -- Set slot cost (lua l.65-66).
            MoneyFrame_Update("PetStableCostMoneyFrame", GetNextStableSlotCost())

            -- Set slot statuseses (lua l.68-119, the reference's own spelling of the comment).
            -- An UNBOUGHT slot is drawn differently from an EMPTY one: bought-and-empty keeps its
            -- white empty-slot art and stays clickable, unbought goes red and disabled. That is the
            -- whole visual difference between "you may stable a pet here" and "buy this first".
            local button
            local background
            local icon, name, level, family, loyalty
            for i = 1, NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS do
                button = getglobal("PetStableStabledPet" .. i)
                background = getglobal("PetStableStabledPet" .. i .. "Background")
                icon, name, level, family, loyalty = GetStablePetInfo(i)
                SetItemButtonTexture(button, icon)
                if i <= GetNumStableSlots() then
                    background:SetVertexColor(1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
                    button:Enable()
                    if icon then
                        button.tooltip = name
                        button.tooltipSubtext = format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), level) .. " " .. family
                    else
                        button.tooltip = EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT
                        button.tooltipSubtext = ""
                    end
                    if i == selectedPet then
                        if icon then
                            button:SetChecked(1)
                            PetStableLevelText:SetText(
                                name .. " " .. format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), level) .. " " .. family)
                            PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText(loyalty)
                            SetPetStablePaperdoll(PetStableModel)
                            PetStablePetInfo.tooltip =
                                format(PET_DIET_TEMPLATE, BuildListString(GetStablePetFoodTypes(i)))
                            if not PetStableModel:IsShown() then
                                PetStableModel:Show()
                            end
                        else
                            button:SetChecked(nil)
                            PetStableLevelText:SetText("")
                            PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText("")
                            PetStableModel:Hide()
                        end
                    else
                        button:SetChecked(nil)
                    end
                    -- lua l.109-114: if this button's tooltip is the one currently up, rebuild it
                    -- in place. A repaint that changed a slot under a resting cursor would
                    -- otherwise leave the previous pet's name on screen.
                    if GameTooltip:IsOwned(button) then
                        GameTooltip:SetOwner(button, "ANCHOR_RIGHT")
                        GameTooltip:SetText(button.tooltip)
                        GameTooltip:AddLine(button.tooltipSubtext, "", 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
                        GameTooltip:Show()
                    end
                else
                    background:SetVertexColor(1.0, 0.1, 0.1)
                    button:Disable()
                end
            end

            -- Current pet slot (lua l.121-159). Three cases, and the middle one is the interesting
            -- one: a hunter whose pet is dismissed still has a slot-0 row from the server (read off
            -- the character-pet cache), so the reference falls back to `GetStablePetInfo(0)` rather
            -- than showing an empty current-pet slot for a pet that merely is not out.
            if selectedPet == 0 then
                if UnitExists("pet") then
                    PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(1)
                    local family = UnitCreatureFamily("pet")
                    if not family then
                        family = ""
                    end
                    PetStableLevelText:SetText(
                        UnitName("pet") .. " " .. format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), UnitLevel("pet"))
                        .. " " .. family)
                    PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText(GetPetLoyalty())
                    SetPetStablePaperdoll(PetStableModel)
                    if not PetStableModel:IsShown() then
                        PetStableModel:Show()
                    end
                    if GetPetFoodTypes() then
                        PetStablePetInfo.tooltip =
                            format(PET_DIET_TEMPLATE, BuildListString(GetPetFoodTypes()))
                    end
                elseif GetStablePetInfo(0) then
                    -- If pet doesn't exist it might be dismissed, so check stable slot 0 for
                    -- current pet info (lua l.139, the reference's own comment).
                    PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(1)
                    icon, name, level, family, loyalty = GetStablePetInfo(0)
                    PetStableLevelText:SetText(
                        name .. " " .. format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), level) .. " " .. family)
                    PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText(loyalty)
                    SetPetStablePaperdoll(PetStableModel)
                    if not PetStableModel:IsShown() then
                        PetStableModel:Show()
                    end
                    if GetStablePetFoodTypes(0) then
                        PetStablePetInfo.tooltip =
                            format(PET_DIET_TEMPLATE, BuildListString(GetStablePetFoodTypes(0)))
                    end
                else
                    PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(nil)
                    PetStableLevelText:SetText("")
                    PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText("")
                    PetStableModel:Hide()
                end
            else
                PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(nil)
            end

            -- Set tooltip and icon info (lua l.160-181). The same live/dismissed/absent ladder as
            -- the block above, for the BUTTON's own icon and hover rather than the pane.
            if GetPetIcon() then
                SetItemButtonTexture(PetStableCurrentPet, GetPetIcon())
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltip = UnitName("pet")
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltipSubtext =
                    format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), UnitLevel("pet")) .. " " .. UnitCreatureFamily("pet")
            elseif GetStablePetInfo(0) then
                icon, name, level, family, loyalty = GetStablePetInfo(0)
                SetItemButtonTexture(PetStableCurrentPet, icon)
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltip = name
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltipSubtext =
                    format(TEXT(UNIT_LEVEL_TEMPLATE), level) .. " " .. family
            else
                SetItemButtonTexture(PetStableCurrentPet, "")
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltip = EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT
                PetStableCurrentPet.tooltipSubtext = ""
                PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(nil)
            end
            if GameTooltip:IsOwned(PetStableCurrentPet) then
                GameTooltip:SetOwner(PetStableCurrentPet, "ANCHOR_RIGHT")
                GameTooltip:SetText(PetStableCurrentPet.tooltip)
                GameTooltip:AddLine(PetStableCurrentPet.tooltipSubtext, "", 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
                GameTooltip:Show()
            end

            -- If no selected pet clear everything out (lua l.183-189). Reachable even after the
            -- auto-selection above: a hunter with no pet and an empty stable has nothing to select.
            if selectedPet == -1 then
                -- no pet
                PetStableModel:Hide()
                PetStableLevelText:SetText("")
                PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText("")
            end

            -- Enable, disable, or hide purchase button (lua l.191-208). Three states, and the first
            -- is a HIDE of the whole row: with both slots bought there is nothing left to sell, so
            -- the button, the "Cost:" label, its coins and the slot-purchase line all go. Below
            -- that the button only enables when the player can afford it, and the coins turn red
            -- when they cannot. The leading unconditional Show() is the reference's own and it is
            -- what brings the button back after a NoPetsAllowed() pass hid it.
            PetStablePurchaseButton:Show()
            if GetNumStableSlots() == NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS then
                PetStablePurchaseButton:Hide()
                PetStableCostLabel:Hide()
                PetStableCostMoneyFrame:Hide()
                PetStableSlotText:Hide()
            elseif GetMoney() >= GetNextStableSlotCost() then
                PetStablePurchaseButton:Enable()
                PetStableCostLabel:Show()
                PetStableCostMoneyFrame:Show()
                SetMoneyFrameColor("PetStableCostMoneyFrame",
                    HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.r, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.g, HIGHLIGHT_FONT_COLOR.b)
            else
                PetStablePurchaseButton:Disable()
                PetStableCostLabel:Show()
                PetStableCostMoneyFrame:Show()
                SetMoneyFrameColor("PetStableCostMoneyFrame",
                    RED_FONT_COLOR.r, RED_FONT_COLOR.g, RED_FONT_COLOR.b)
            end
        end

        -- lua l.211-229 — the warlock face of the window, called from BOTH the paperdoll event arm
        -- and the top of Update. It is not "clear the window": it is the window with nothing on
        -- offer — every slot unchecked and tooltipped as empty, the pane hidden, and the whole
        -- purchase row gone, because a warlock cannot buy a stable slot either.
        function PetStable_NoPetsAllowed()
            local button
            for i = 1, NUM_PET_STABLE_SLOTS do
                button = getglobal("PetStableStabledPet" .. i)
                button.tooltip = EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT
                button:SetChecked(nil)
            end

            PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(nil)
            PetStableLevelText:SetText("")
            PetStableLoyaltyText:SetText("")
            PetStableModel:Hide()
            SetItemButtonTexture(PetStableCurrentPet, "")
            PetStableCurrentPet.tooltip = EMPTY_STABLE_SLOT
            PetStableCurrentPet:SetChecked(nil)
            PetStablePurchaseButton:Hide()
            PetStableCostLabel:Hide()
            PetStableCostMoneyFrame:Hide()
            PetStableSlotText:Hide()
        end
    ]]></Script>

    <!--
        One slot button (xml l.4-71): a 37x37 CheckButton wearing the standard quickslot ring, with
        the empty-slot art behind it and the pet's icon in front. `$parentIconTexture` is the name
        `SetItemButtonTexture` addresses (ItemButtonTemplate.xml's naming contract), and
        `$parentBackground` is the one Update tints red for an unbought slot.

        **`ClickStablePet` is wired to BOTH `OnClick` and `OnReceiveDrag`, and the frame repaints
        only when it returns true.** That is the reference's own arrangement and it is transcribed
        exactly, deliberately: the binding carries two jobs behind one name (commit a drag if one is
        in flight, otherwise select) and its drag half is still being reverse-engineered
        (`benilla-ui/src/script/stable.rs`'s `drag_verb`, INFERRED). Baking an assumption about
        which it does into this XML would put the wrong answer in two places instead of one, so the
        XML asks the same question the reference asks and repaints on the same answer.

        `RegisterForDrag("LeftButton")` is what arms `OnDragStart` on a press-and-move; the pickup
        it starts is FRAME-LOCAL, not the global cursor (there is no stable-pet cursor mode in
        1.12 — stable.rs's module doc), so nothing else on screen can be holding a stable pet.
    -->
    <CheckButton name="PetStableSlotTemplate" virtual="true">
        <Size><AbsDimension x="37" y="37"/></Size>
        <Layers>
            <Layer level="BORDER">
                <Texture name="$parentIconTexture"/>
            </Layer>
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <Texture name="$parentBackground" file="Interface\Buttons\UI-EmptySlot">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="64" y="64"/></Size>
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-13" y="13"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </Texture>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>this:RegisterForDrag("LeftButton")</OnLoad>
            <OnEnter>
                -- xml l.31-38. Guarded on `this.tooltip` because Update has not necessarily run
                -- yet: a hover before the first repaint has nothing to say and says nothing.
                if this.tooltip then
                    GameTooltip:SetOwner(this, "ANCHOR_RIGHT")
                    GameTooltip:SetText(this.tooltip)
                    GameTooltip:AddLine(this.tooltipSubtext, "", 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)
                    GameTooltip:Show()
                end
            </OnEnter>
            <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
            <OnClick>
                if ClickStablePet(this:GetID()) then
                    PetStable_Update()
                end
            </OnClick>
            <OnDragStart>PickupStablePet(this:GetID())</OnDragStart>
            <OnReceiveDrag>
                if ClickStablePet(this:GetID()) then
                    PetStable_Update()
                end
            </OnReceiveDrag>
        </Scripts>
        <NormalTexture name="$parentNormalTexture" file="Interface\Buttons\UI-Quickslot2">
            <Size><AbsDimension x="64" y="64"/></Size>
            <Anchors>
                <Anchor point="CENTER"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-1"/></Offset></Anchor>
            </Anchors>
        </NormalTexture>
        <PushedTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-Quickslot-Depress"/>
        <HighlightTexture file="Interface\Buttons\ButtonHilight-Square" alphaMode="ADD"/>
        <CheckedTexture file="Interface\Buttons\CheckButtonHilight" alphaMode="ADD"/>
    </CheckButton>

    <Frame name="PetStableFrame" parent="UIParent" toplevel="true" movable="true" enableMouse="true" hidden="true">
        <!-- xml l.72-85: the standard 384x512 panel window. The HitRectInsets pull the mouse rect
             in from the art's transparent right edge and bottom skirt. -->
        <Size><AbsDimension x="384" y="512"/></Size>
        <Anchors>
            <Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-104"/></Offset></Anchor>
        </Anchors>
        <HitRectInsets><AbsInset left="0" right="34" top="0" bottom="75"/></HitRectInsets>
        <Layers>
            <!-- The stable master's portrait, 60x60 TOPLEFT (7,-6) (xml l.88-99). Bound in Update
                 via SetPortraitTexture(region, "npc") — the live model bake of whoever we are
                 talking to, the same binding every NPC-session window here uses. -->
            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                <Texture name="PetStableFramePortrait">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="60" y="60"/></Size>
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="7" y="-6"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </Texture>
            </Layer>
            <Layer level="BORDER">
                <!-- The four quadrant slabs that ARE the window art (xml l.102-133): 256-wide left
                     halves, 128-wide right halves, each 256 tall, pinned to their own corner. -->
                <Texture file="Interface\PetStableFrame\UI-PetStable-TopLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture file="Interface\PetStableFrame\UI-PetStable-TopRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="TOPRIGHT"/></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture name="PetStableFrameBottomLeft" file="Interface\PetStableFrame\UI-PetStable-BottomLeft">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="256" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT"/></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <Texture name="PetStableFrameBottomRight" file="Interface\PetStableFrame\UI-PetStable-BottomRight">
                    <Size><AbsDimension x="128" y="256"/></Size>
                    <Anchors><Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT"/></Anchors>
                </Texture>
                <!-- Title (xml l.134-142). -->
                <FontString name="PetStableTitleLabel" inherits="GameFontHighlight" text="STABLES">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-18"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <!-- The selected pet's name/level/family line and its loyalty line (xml l.143-160).
                     The reference ships them carrying PLACEHOLDER text ("Bobby The Fleshripper
                     (Level 99 Raptor)", "Loyalty: Mans Best Friend") — an authoring artifact that
                     is on screen for the instant before the first Update, and one this engine would
                     render literally (a `text=` that misses the global string table falls back to
                     the literal). Dropped: the strings are Update's to write. -->
                <FontString name="PetStableLevelText" inherits="GameFontNormal">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP" relativeTo="PetStableTitleLabel" relativePoint="BOTTOM">
                            <Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-8"/></Offset>
                        </Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="PetStableLoyaltyText" inherits="GameFontNormalSmall">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="TOP" relativeTo="PetStableLevelText" relativePoint="BOTTOM">
                            <Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="-1"/></Offset>
                        </Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <!-- "Do you wish to purchase another stable slot?" and the "Cost:" label
                     (xml l.161-178) — both hidden by Update once both slots are bought. -->
                <FontString name="PetStableSlotText" inherits="GameFontHighlightSmall" text="STABLE_SLOT_TEXT">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="BOTTOM"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-10" y="132"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
                <FontString name="PetStableCostLabel" inherits="GameFontNormal" text="COSTS_LABEL">
                    <Anchors>
                        <Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="74" y="110"/></Offset></Anchor>
                    </Anchors>
                </FontString>
            </Layer>
        </Layers>
        <Frames>
            <!-- The model pane (ref `<PlayerModel name="PetStableModel">`, xml l.182-280): 313x223
                 at TOPLEFT (23,-76), the reference's own widget type since decision 1751 gave
                 `<Model>`/`<PlayerModel>` panes a draw of their own (portrait/mod.rs's
                 `MODEL_PANE_BOOTHS`).

                 The `$parentTexture` this used to carry is gone with it, and so is decision 1070's
                 BACKGROUND-not-ARTWORK note: that workaround existed because a booth *texture* is
                 an opaque region in a BUCKET-WIDE draw layer that outranks the frame (0884), so at
                 ARTWORK it painted over the diet icon below. The pane's own draw slot has no such
                 problem — it emits at the FRAME's z, which is what puts every child above it, and
                 that is the reference's own layering rather than a tie we had to arrange.

                 The pane keeps its reference NAME because the Lua passes it by name to
                 `SetPetStablePaperdoll` at four sites, an addon may too, and the app now joins the
                 booth to the pane by that name. -->
            <PlayerModel name="PetStableModel">
                <Size><AbsDimension x="313" y="223"/></Size>
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="23" y="-76"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Frames>
                    <!-- Rotate buttons (xml l.206-247): 35x35, the left one at the pane's own
                         TOPLEFT and the right one chained off its TOPRIGHT, calling the
                         reference's own `Model_RotateLeft`/`Model_RotateRight` (UIParent.xml). -->
                    <Button name="PetStableModelRotateLeftButton">
                        <Size><AbsDimension x="35" y="35"/></Size>
                        <Anchors>
                            <Anchor point="TOPLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="0"/></Offset></Anchor>
                        </Anchors>
                        <NormalTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-RotationLeft-Button-Up"/>
                        <PushedTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-RotationLeft-Button-Down"/>
                        <HighlightTexture file="Interface\Buttons\ButtonHilight-Round" alphaMode="ADD"/>
                        <Scripts>
                            <OnLoad>this:RegisterForClicks("LeftButtonDown", "LeftButtonUp")</OnLoad>
                            <OnClick>Model_RotateLeft(this:GetParent())</OnClick>
                        </Scripts>
                    </Button>
                    <Button name="PetStableModelRotateRightButton">
                        <Size><AbsDimension x="35" y="35"/></Size>
                        <Anchors>
                            <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="PetStableModelRotateLeftButton" relativePoint="TOPRIGHT"/>
                        </Anchors>
                        <NormalTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-RotationRight-Button-Up"/>
                        <PushedTexture file="Interface\Buttons\UI-RotationRight-Button-Down"/>
                        <HighlightTexture file="Interface\Buttons\ButtonHilight-Round" alphaMode="ADD"/>
                        <Scripts>
                            <OnLoad>this:RegisterForClicks("LeftButtonDown", "LeftButtonUp")</OnLoad>
                            <OnClick>Model_RotateRight(this:GetParent())</OnClick>
                        </Scripts>
                    </Button>
                    <!-- The diet icon (xml l.248-278): 24x23 under the rotate buttons, its texcoords
                         STATIC. This is the pet's DIET affordance (its tooltip lists what it eats),
                         not a happiness readout — the happiness face that does move lives on the pet
                         unit frame (decisions 1005/0990), and PetPaperDollFrame.xml's twin carries
                         the same note.

                         Kept as a CHILD of the pane, where the reference has it, and not lifted to a
                         sibling the way the pet paper doll's was: Update hides the pane whenever
                         there is no pet to show, and being a child is what makes the icon go with
                         it. Declared after the pane's own quad, so at the shared BACKGROUND layer
                         the link stamp draws it on top. -->
                    <Frame name="PetStablePetInfo" enableMouse="true" hidden="false">
                        <Size><AbsDimension x="24" y="23"/></Size>
                        <Anchors>
                            <Anchor point="TOPLEFT" relativeTo="PetStableModelRotateLeftButton" relativePoint="BOTTOMLEFT">
                                <Offset><AbsDimension x="7" y="0"/></Offset>
                            </Anchor>
                        </Anchors>
                        <Layers>
                            <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                                <Texture file="Interface\PetPaperDollFrame\UI-PetHappiness" setAllPoints="true">
                                    <Size><AbsDimension x="24" y="23"/></Size>
                                    <TexCoords left="0" right="0.1875" top="0" bottom="0.359375"/>
                                </Texture>
                            </Layer>
                        </Layers>
                        <Scripts>
                            <OnEnter>
                                GameTooltip:SetOwner(this, "ANCHOR_RIGHT")
                                GameTooltip:SetText(this.tooltip)
                            </OnEnter>
                            <OnLeave>GameTooltip:Hide()</OnLeave>
                        </Scripts>
                    </Frame>
                </Frames>
                <Scripts>
                    <!-- The reference's own pair (xml l.194-203): `Model_OnLoad` seeds the 0.61
                         default facing through `SetRotation`, and `Model_OnUpdate` is the
                         held-button continuous spin. The app mirrors this pane's facing onto the
                         "stable" booth each frame (`ui_stable`). -->
                    <OnLoad>Model_OnLoad()</OnLoad>
                    <OnUpdate>Model_OnUpdate(arg1, this)</OnUpdate>
                </Scripts>
            </PlayerModel>

            <!-- The three slots (xml l.281-333). `id=` is what `this:GetID()` reads in the
                 template's handlers, and it IS the binding's slot index: 0 is the pet at your side,
                 1 and 2 the bought stable slots. The two labels are extra regions on the instances,
                 anchored above their buttons; note the reference hangs "Stabled Pets:" off slot 1
                 offset +24 so it centres over the PAIR. -->
            <CheckButton name="PetStableCurrentPet" inherits="PetStableSlotTemplate" id="0">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="BOTTOMLEFT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="95" y="150"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Layers>
                    <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                        <FontString inherits="GameFontNormalSmall" text="CURRENT_PET">
                            <Anchors>
                                <Anchor point="BOTTOM" relativePoint="TOP">
                                    <Offset><AbsDimension x="0" y="6"/></Offset>
                                </Anchor>
                            </Anchors>
                        </FontString>
                    </Layer>
                </Layers>
            </CheckButton>
            <CheckButton name="PetStableStabledPet1" inherits="PetStableSlotTemplate" id="1">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="LEFT" relativeTo="PetStableCurrentPet" relativePoint="RIGHT">
                        <Offset><AbsDimension x="53" y="0"/></Offset>
                    </Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Layers>
                    <Layer level="BACKGROUND">
                        <FontString inherits="GameFontNormalSmall" text="STABLED_PETS">
                            <Anchors>
                                <Anchor point="BOTTOM" relativePoint="TOP">
                                    <Offset><AbsDimension x="24" y="6"/></Offset>
                                </Anchor>
                            </Anchors>
                        </FontString>
                    </Layer>
                </Layers>
            </CheckButton>
            <CheckButton name="PetStableStabledPet2" inherits="PetStableSlotTemplate" id="2">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="LEFT" relativeTo="PetStableStabledPet1" relativePoint="RIGHT">
                        <Offset><AbsDimension x="15" y="0"/></Offset>
                    </Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </CheckButton>

            <!-- Buy the next stable slot (xml l.334-350). The server prices it and picks which slot
                 it is; Update decides whether this button is even on screen. -->
            <Button name="PetStablePurchaseButton" inherits="UIPanelButtonTemplate" text="PURCHASE">
                <Size><AbsDimension x="80" y="22"/></Size>
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-110" y="105"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnClick>BuyStableSlot()</OnClick>
                </Scripts>
            </Button>
            <!-- The player's purse (xml l.351-364). NO <Scripts> here: benilla's
                 SmallMoneyFrameTemplate already wires SmallMoneyFrame_OnLoad as its own OnLoad
                 where the reference's template does not, and an instance block would SetScript OVER
                 it rather than add to it (header, deferral 5). Type PLAYER by default, which is
                 what a purse wants. -->
            <Frame name="PetStableMoneyFrame" inherits="SmallMoneyFrameTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="BOTTOMRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-45" y="84"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Frame>
            <!-- The next slot's price (xml l.365-379): a STATIC frame, so it shows whatever
                 MoneyFrame_Update last parked on it rather than tracking the purse. This one DOES
                 declare an OnLoad, and therefore has to re-call the template's loader beside
                 MoneyFrame_SetType (the AuctionFrame.xml/MailFrame.xml idiom). -->
            <Frame name="PetStableCostMoneyFrame" inherits="SmallMoneyFrameTemplate">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="LEFT" relativeTo="PetStableCostLabel" relativePoint="RIGHT">
                        <Offset><AbsDimension x="5" y="0"/></Offset>
                    </Anchor>
                </Anchors>
                <Scripts>
                    <OnLoad>SmallMoneyFrame_OnLoad() MoneyFrame_SetType("STATIC")</OnLoad>
                </Scripts>
            </Frame>
            <!-- xml l.380-388. UIPanelCloseButton's own OnClick is HideUIPanel(this:GetParent()),
                 and this window's OnHide is what ends the session — so the X needs no handler. -->
            <Button name="PetStableFrameCloseButton" inherits="UIPanelCloseButton">
                <Anchors>
                    <Anchor point="TOPRIGHT"><Offset><AbsDimension x="-29" y="-8"/></Offset></Anchor>
                </Anchors>
            </Button>
        </Frames>
        <!-- xml l.390-404. The NPC-session lifecycle: OnHide fires ClosePetStables() so the session
             ends whenever this window hides for ANY reason (ESC, the X, PET_STABLE_CLOSED, or
             another left-slot window displacing it). Unlike its neighbours that close is purely
             client-side — no packet exists for it in 5875. -->
        <Scripts>
            <OnLoad>PetStable_OnLoad()</OnLoad>
            <OnShow>PlaySound("igCharacterInfoOpen")</OnShow>
            <OnHide>ClosePetStables() PlaySound("igCharacterInfoClose")</OnHide>
            <OnEvent>PetStable_OnEvent()</OnEvent>
        </Scripts>
    </Frame>
</Ui>
